<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NAACP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People is an American civil rights organisation founded in 1909 by an interracial group including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, Mary White Ovington and Ida B. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> to advance justice for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>. The NAACP campaigned against lynching and disenfranchisement through publicity, lobbying and lawsuits, and Du Bois edited its magazine, The Crisis, from 1910. Its legal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, headed by Charles Hamilton Houston and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgood-marshall">Thurgood Marshall</a>, waged a decades-long campaign against the &quot;separate but equal&quot; doctrine that culminated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education</a> in 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naacp.jpg" alt="National Association for the Advancement of Colored People" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: New York World-Telegram and the Sun staff photographer: Al Ravenna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAACP_leaders_with_poster_NYWTS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
